    "prompt": "Below is a description of the Python function `TCPConnector._start_tls_connection`.\n\nDescription:\n\"\"\"\nThe `_start_tls_connection` function is an asynchronous method in the `TCPConnector` class of the `aiohttp` library. It is responsible for initiating a TLS (Transport Layer Security) connection over an existing transport, typically used to upgrade a plain-text connection to a secure one. The function takes four parameters: `underlying_transport`, which is the existing transport to upgrade; `req`, a `ClientRequest` object containing details about the request; `timeout`, a `ClientTimeout` object specifying how long to wait for the TLS handshake; and `client_error`, an optional exception class to use for client errors, defaulting to `ClientConnectorError`.\n\nThe function first creates a new protocol instance for TLS using the `_factory` method. It then retrieves an SSL context using the `_get_ssl_context` method, which is necessary for the TLS handshake. If the SSL context is `None`, an assertion error is raised, although this situation is unreachable in the current code path.\n\nThe function attempts to start the TLS handshake using the `start_tls` method of the event loop, passing the underlying transport, the TLS protocol, the SSL context, and other relevant parameters like the server hostname and handshake timeout. If an exception occurs during this process, the underlying transport is closed, and the exception is re-raised. If the TLS transport is successfully created, the function checks the server's fingerprint using the `_get_fingerprint` method. If the fingerprint does not match, the TLS transport is closed, and a `ServerFingerprintMismatch` exception is raised.\n\nThe function handles various exceptions, including certificate errors, SSL errors, OS errors, and type errors, raising specific exceptions like `ClientConnectorCertificateError`, `ClientConnectorSSLError`, and `ClientConnectionError` with detailed error messages. If the TLS transport is `None` after the handshake attempt, an `OSError` is raised.\n\nFinally, the function returns a tuple containing the TLS transport and the TLS protocol. It also calls the `connection_made` method on the TLS protocol to kickstart the state machine of the new TLS protocol. This method has side effects, including potentially closing transports and appending them to a cleanup list if necessary.\n\"\"\"\n\nSplit the description into atomic claims. Each claim must be a single, self-contained factual statement about the function that could be checked against the function's source code (name the function explicitly; no pronouns referring to other claims). Do not add claims that are not in the description; do not merge two facts into one claim. Keep the original wording where possible. Assign each claim exactly one type:\n- \"structural\": the function's signature, parameter names/defaults/types, return type, or which functions, methods, classes or attributes it uses.\n- \"dataflow\": how a value is computed, transformed, passed on, or what is returned for which input.\n- \"behavioural\": exceptions raised or caught, conditions/early returns, side effects, edge-case handling, ordering/iteration behaviour.\n- \"intent\": purpose, rationale, or the role of the function in the larger system.\n\nReturn a JSON array of at most 12 objects: [{\"text\": \"...\", \"type\": \"structural|dataflow|behavioural|intent\"}, ...]",
